README-CIO -- the starred programs, and the five modules that serve them=======================================================================Some of the names in DOC/INDEX carry a star.  A star means the program needsone of Microware's runtime trap modules: `cio` for nearly all of them, andfor some `math`, `math881`, `csl` or `csl020`.THEY RUN HERE.  All five modules are on this disk, in CMDS, included withMicroware's permission -- SOURCES.txt records the exchange.  You need donothing, supply nothing, and fetch nothing.  The star tells you what aprogram depends on, not what it lacks.This file explains what the star means, and what to do in the two caseswhere you might want a different arrangement.Why so many programs need it----------------------------`cc -qixm` -- the C library in a shared trap handler rather than linked intoevery binary -- was the normal way to build for OS-9, and it is why a starredprogram is often a fraction of the size of a trap-free one.  Much of thiscollection was rebuilt trap-free (`-qm`) so it would run with nothing loadedat all; most of the programs under CMDS need no module whatever.  The restwere left as their authors built them.A program that cannot find its trap handler stops at once with    **** Can't install trap handler ****That is the message to recognise.  It is not a broken program, and on thisdisk you should never see it -- if you do, the five modules have been removedor the execution directory is not this disk's CMDS.The list was MEASURED, by running every program on a disk with the fivemodules taken away and matching that message.  It was never derived fromlooking for the string `cio` inside a binary: that proxy is wrong in bothdirections, and this collection has the scars to prove it -- `vi_nocio`contains the string and does not need the module, while `cyberwar`, `gnuchess`and `g` lack it and do.If you would rather use your own--------------------------------You run OS-9, so you have these modules already, and you may prefer your copy-- a different edition, or one matched to your processor.  Two ways:1. KEEP YOUR OWN /dd.  Point os9exec at your system disk and mount this one   alongside, so /dd stays yours and the collection is simply another drive:       OS9DISK=/path/to/your/os9disk  OS9H1=/path/to/freeware/dd  os9exec shell       chx /h1/CMDS   Your `cio` is found first, from your own /dd/CMDS.2. REPLACE THE COPIES HERE.  Overwrite them in this disk's CMDS:       copy /h1/CMDS/cio  /dd/CMDS/cio       copy /h1/CMDS/math /dd/CMDS/math   The disk you end up with then contains modules from your own SDK, so keep   that copy for your own use; the collection as published stays as it is.3. LOAD IT, wherever it is.  A trap handler has to be reachable through   the EXECUTION directory, which is why the message appears at all when   you run from somewhere else.  Putting the module in the module   directory first settles it wherever you are:       load /dd/CMDS/cio   CMDS/load is on this disk (source in SRC/load), so this needs nothing   fetched.  It is also how the six RTF Fortran programs get `os9lib' --   DOC/README-FORTRAN.   MEASURED 2026-08-28, both ways round: with the execution directory   moved to a directory holding no cio, `banner HI' stops with the   message above; after `load /dd/CMDS/cio' in the same session it   draws its banner.`math` is needed only by the programs that do floating point -- seeDOC/README-FLOATINGPOINT, which also explains when you want `math881`instead.DOC/README-RUNNING covers where the collection should be mounted, and thehandful of programs that look for files under /h0.If you take the modules away----------------------------Someone redistributing a subset, or testing what is genuinely free-standing,may want the disk without them.  Remove all five from CMDS and most of theprograms still run -- that is the whole point of the rebuild work.  Thestarred ones stop with the message above until a licensed `cio` is put back.Where a starred program mattered, there is usually a trap-free alternativealready here:    starred           use instead    -------           -----------    *ed  *emacs       vi, sedt, sed    *vi_cio           vi (the EFFO build)    *ksh              bash, sh, gshell    *zoo              lha, tar, gzip    *fgrep            grep    *ckermit          kermit    *cat.cio          cat    *rcs and friends  no free replacement on this diskDOC/INDEX marks every one of them, so you can tell before you try.A FEW cio PROGRAMS FAIL EVEN WITH cio PRESENT -- and here is how to tell------------------------------------------------------------------------Two programs on this disk answer with hundreds of lines of    No more memory !!!and do their job not at all:    echo 255 | cvtbase d h    setenv PORT /term ; logisim /dd/DOC/logisim/flipflop.lsiThat message comes from the EMULATOR, not from the program, which is why itreads like a broken machine.  It is neither.  Root cause found 2026-08-31:  A cio program reaches the C library through TRAP #13 with a selector.  The  archives here were linked against a `cio.l' whose table has `_flshbuf' at  selector $41.  Every `cio' MODULE anyone has -- ours, the SDK's -- puts a  MEMORY routine there instead.  So the program's `putc' calls what it thinks  is "flush this FILE" and reaches "allocate this many bytes", handing over  the FILE's ADDRESS as the byte count.  The allocation succeeds, the FILE is  never updated, and the next character does it again.  A few hundred  thousand times.  The cio module that would match those binaries is on none of these disks.WHAT THIS MEANS WHEN YOU MEET IT.  No amount of memory helps, and the binarycannot be repaired -- but the PROGRAM usually can, because a `-qm' build fromthe same source never enters cio at all.  Sixteen have been rebuilt thatway and every one of them works.  Use the alternative in the table above forthe rest.WHICH PROGRAMS.  333 modules here link cio; 24 contain the call that can dothis.  SIXTEEN MORE DID, AND WERE REBUILT ON 2026-08-31 -- they are on thisdisk now as trap-free `-qm' builds, and every one of them works:    cvtbase   logisim   unstr   cdiff   pagekwic   pagefraz    nroff     etags     cookhash   yacc   xrf    printf    valspeak  unifdef   ape    hexedAND CARRYING THE CALL IS NOT THE SAME AS MAKING IT.  This is the thing toknow before writing any of the remaining twenty-four off.  Every one of thetwenty-nine that carried a call site on the morning of 2026-08-31 was runthat evening with real arguments and real files, and TWENTY-FOUR OF THEM DIDTHEIR WORK CORRECTLY -- `ascii' writes its whole 4,290-byte table, `diff' and`spiff' both produce a diff, `strfile' builds a fortune index, `makelex'compiles a word list, `loan' prints a full amortisation schedule, `crypto',`snap', `vis', `lp', `undel', `dam', `sedt', `wish', `rpoem', `newsgen',`blackjak', `poker', `stone' and `tess' all answer properly.  The scan is anUPPER BOUND and it is a loose one: a call site in a function nothing callscannot storm, and most of these are in error paths.Only five of the twenty-nine actually misbehaved, and those five are theones rebuilt above.  Do not read a name off the scan and conclude a programis broken; run it.AND THE OTHER DIRECTION IS A TRAP TOO.  `setfont' looked like a sixth: itwrites no byte for a real font file, to /term or to $PORT or to a file$PORT names, and never reaches its own `Can't open' message.  It wasrebuilt and installed on that evidence and then TAKEN BACK OUT, because the`-qm' build does exactly the same thing -- which is what rules the mismatchout.  Silence is not a diagnosis.  A rebuild that changes nothing is thecheapest way to tell the two apart, and it is worth doing before replacingan archive binary with one of ours.Each was built from the source and the recipe this repository alreadycarried, `cc <src> -qm=16k -DOSK -n=<name>', and each is larger for it --`cvtbase' went from 4,666 bytes to 16,476 -- which is the whole trade the`-qixm' link was making.  Measured after the rebuild: `cvtbase d h' answers`ff' for 255 where it used to storm; `cdiff' produces a context diff where itused to say "MAXLINECOUNT exceeded"; `pagekwic' and `pagefraz' produce a realkeyword-in-context index where they used to say "word too long in line 0";`etags' writes a tags file; `yacc' writes y.tab.c; `nroff' formats.Two of the eleven want something else besides and now say which: `xrf' wantsits language table `C.XRF' in the DATA directory (it is on this disk, asDOC/xrf/c.xrf), and `logisim' wants the `PORT' environment variable set.HOW THEY USED TO FAIL, kept because it is how you will recognise one of theremaining twenty-five if it ever does fire:  LOUDLY -- hundreds of `No more memory !!!' and no work done.  SILENTLY -- the program opens your file, reads NOT ONE BYTE of it, and  reports on it anyway.  `cdiff' said "MAXLINECOUNT exceeded" on a three-line  file; `pagekwic' said "word too long in line 0" on eighteen bytes whose  longest word is five; `nroff' and `etags' printed nothing at all;  `cookhash' printed "000000".  Each looks like a program with a small bug  rather than one that never read its input.SEEN IN ONE RUN.  `xrf' was the clearest case, because it read one filecorrectly and never read the other in the same process:    I$Open  "cdiff.c"     <- the source, 30K    I$Open  "C.XRF"       <- the language table    I$Read  the table                          and it printed "Language table                                               loaded"    ...and cdiff.c was NEVER READ.  Not one byte.The table went through `read()' and arrived intact.  The source went throughthe `getc' macro and never arrived at all.  Same program, same run, two files.AND BEWARE THE MESSAGE ITSELF.  A program can print `No more memory !!!' onceor twice for the ordinary reason: it asked for memory and there was none.`sedt' does, and it is not broken by this defect.  Nor is the message reliableany more -- os9exec counts refused allocations instead of printing them all,as of 2026-08-31, so a storming program is now SILENT and simply neverreturns.  What tells you is a file opened and never read.The call only fires if the program runs a `putc' or `getc' MACRO on a FILE.Anything that prints through `printf', `fprintf' or `fwrite' is unaffected --which is why every one of these prints its own usage text perfectly, and whythe starred programs pass 272,000 bytes of input through with no trouble.THE THIRTY ARE NOT A GUESS.  Two versions of Microware's `cio.l' exist, and abinary shows which one it linked: the highest trap-13 selector in its own stubtable.  EVERY ONE of them is from the $44 group and NOT ONE program from the$45 group is affected.  The modules we have dispatch $00..$45, so the $45library is the matching one.  In it, `putc' and `getc' are ordinary functioncalls handled inside the module; in the $44 library they are macros whose slowpath calls the broken selectors.  Nothing has to be run to know which group aprogram is in.A WARNING FOR ANYONE RELINKING FOR SIZE.  `tools/rebuild/relink_cio.sh' turnsa `-qm' build back into a `-qixm' one to save space, and `-qixm' links the SDKcopy of `cio.l' -- WHICH IS THE $44 LIBRARY.  So a relinked program is a $44program, and if it runs a putc/getc macro it breaks exactly like these thirty.`tools/cio_macro_scan.py' is the gate: relink where it is silent about theresult, and build `-qm' where it is not.  `CMDS/REBUILT/kermit_cio' is in thelist below for exactly this reason -- it is a relink that carries the callsites.The thirty, most call sites first.  They carry the call and may never run it,so this is a list to be careful with rather than a list of broken programs:    CMDS/hexed              CMDS/liborder           CMDS/unpacklib    CMDS/sedt               CMDS/GAMES/valspeak     CMDS/diff    CMDS/loan               CMDS/makelex            CMDS/REBUILT/kermit_cio    CMDS/GAMES/newsgen      CMDS/chksum             CMDS/crypto    CMDS/dam                CMDS/snap               CMDS/spiff    CMDS/GAMES/blackjak     CMDS/GAMES/poker        CMDS/GAMES/stone    CMDS/ape                CMDS/ascii              CMDS/printf    CMDS/undel              CMDS/unifdef            CMDS/wish    CMDS/GAMES/tess         CMDS/lp                 CMDS/setfont    CMDS/strfile            CMDS/vis                CMDS/GAMES/rpoemIf you meet it in a program not named here, that is the shape to recognise:usage text fine, real work impossible, and the emulator's voice in place ofthe program's.tools/cio_macro_scan.py regenerates this list, and check_disk fails if thefigures above stop matching the disk.